## Proof

**Key Lemma.** *If $f \in \mathbb{C}[x_1, \dots, x_n]$ vanishes on all of $\mathbb{Z}^n$, then $f \equiv 0$.*

**Proof of Lemma (by induction on $n$).**

*Base case ($n = 1$).* Let $f \in \mathbb{C}[x]$ vanish on $\mathbb{Z}$. If $f \neq 0$, then $f$ has degree $d \geq 0$ and at most $d$ roots in $\mathbb{C}$. But $f$ vanishes on the infinite set $\mathbb{Z}$, a contradiction. Hence $f \equiv 0$.

*Inductive step.* Assume the lemma holds for $n-1$ variables. Let $f \in \mathbb{C}[x_1, \dots, x_n]$ vanish on $\mathbb{Z}^n$. View $f$ as a polynomial in $x_n$ with coefficients in $\mathbb{C}[x_1, \dots, x_{n-1}]$:

$$f = \sum_{k=0}^{d} g_k(x_1, \dots, x_{n-1})\, x_n^k, \qquad g_k \in \mathbb{C}[x_1, \dots, x_{n-1}].$$

Fix any $(a_1, \dots, a_{n-1}) \in \mathbb{Z}^{n-1}$. Then the single-variable polynomial

$$h(x_n) := f(a_1, \dots, a_{n-1}, x_n) = \sum_{k=0}^{d} g_k(a_1, \dots, a_{n-1})\, x_n^k$$

vanishes at every $a_n \in \mathbb{Z}$, since $(a_1, \dots, a_{n-1}, a_n) \in \mathbb{Z}^n$. By the base case, $h \equiv 0$, so

$$g_k(a_1, \dots, a_{n-1}) = 0 \quad \text{for all } k = 0, \dots, d.$$

This holds for every $(a_1, \dots, a_{n-1}) \in \mathbb{Z}^{n-1}$, so each $g_k$ vanishes on $\mathbb{Z}^{n-1}$. By the inductive hypothesis, $g_k \equiv 0$ for all $k$. Therefore $f \equiv 0$. $\square$

---

**Main Proof.**

Since $L \subseteq \mathbb{C}^n$ is an algebraic set, by definition $L = V(S)$ for some subset $S \subseteq \mathbb{C}[x_1, \dots, x_n]$. Because $\mathbb{C}[x_1, \dots, x_n]$ is Noetherian, the ideal $(S)$ is finitely generated, so there exist $f_1, \dots, f_m \in \mathbb{C}[x_1, \dots, x_n]$ with

$$L = V(f_1, \dots, f_m) = \{ z \in \mathbb{C}^n : f_i(z) = 0 \text{ for all } i \}.$$

The hypothesis $\mathbb{Z}^n \subseteq L$ means that for every $i \in \{1, \dots, m\}$ and every $a \in \mathbb{Z}^n$, we have $f_i(a) = 0$. In other words, each $f_i$ vanishes on all of $\mathbb{Z}^n$.

By the Key Lemma, $f_i \equiv 0$ for every $i$. Consequently,

$$L = V(f_1, \dots, f_m) = V(0, \dots, 0) = \mathbb{C}^n.$$

Therefore $L = \mathbb{C}^n$, as required. $\blacksquare$

